faq:
  - q: "Should you send a thank you email after an interview?"
    a: "Yes — always. Not because it guarantees an outcome, but because not sending one is occasionally noticed negatively, and sending one well reinforces your candidacy. The email is most impactful when it references something specific from the conversation — not just a generic 'thank you for your time.'"
  - q: "When should you send a thank you email after an interview?"
    a: "Within 24 hours of the interview, ideally the same day. Hiring decisions at fast-moving companies sometimes happen within 48 hours of the final interview. Waiting 3–4 days means the email may arrive after a decision has been made."
  - q: "What should a thank you email include?"
    a: "Four elements: (1) Brief thanks for the conversation. (2) One specific reference to something discussed — shows you were listening. (3) One brief reinforcement of your fit — tie your strongest relevant experience to something the interviewer emphasized. (4) A clear close — 'Looking forward to next steps.' Do not write more than 150–200 words."
  - q: "Should you send a thank you email to every interviewer?"
    a: "Yes — personalized to each. Panel interviews may involve 3–5 interviewers. Each should get a distinct email that references something specific about their part of the conversation. Generic copies of the same email to multiple interviewers at the same company look lazy if compared."
